.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt MOUNT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mount ,
.Nm umount2
.Nd attach or detach a file system
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/mount.h
.Ft int
.Fn mount "const char *source" "const char *target" "const char *type" "unsigned long flags" "const void *data"
.Ft int
.Fn umount2 "const char *target" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn mount
attaches the file system on
.Fa source
at the directory
.Fa target .
.Fa type
selects the file-system type,
.Fa flags
supplies mount flags, and
.Fa data
points to a null-terminated option string.
A null
.Fa data
is treated as an empty string.
Both calls require effective user ID 0.
.Pp
The supported types are
.Ql vfat ,
.Ql exfat ,
.Ql ext2 ,
.Ql krffs ,
and
.Ql tmpfs .
.Dv MS_RDONLY ,
.Dv MS_NOSUID ,
.Dv MS_NOEXEC ,
and
.Dv MS_NODEV
are enforced;
.Dv MS_NODEV
makes an open of a device node on the mount return
.Er EACCES .
.Dv MS_SYNCHRONOUS
and
.Dv MS_DIRSYNC
make ext2 put each metadata write on the medium before the next, with a
flush of the disk's cache; without them the writes reach the disk in order
and its cache is flushed at
.Xr sync 2 ,
.Xr fsync 2 ,
unmount and the periodic sync, as on Linux.
.Dv MS_NOATIME ,
.Dv MS_NODIRATIME ,
.Dv MS_RELATIME ,
and
.Dv MS_SILENT
are recorded in the mount table.
.Dv MS_REMOUNT
gives the mount whose root
.Fa target
names the other flags, and reads neither
.Fa source ,
.Fa filesystemtype
nor
.Fa data .
On ext2 it makes the mount read-only, synced and marked clean as an
unmount leaves it, or writable, checked as a writable mount is; tmpfs
changes between the two at once; KRFFS, FAT32 and exFAT refuse a change
between them with
.Er EINVAL
and take one of the other flags.
A mount is not made read-only while a descriptor is open on it for
writing, or on ext2 while a file removed from it is open,
.Er EBUSY ,
and is not made writable on a device that refuses writes or has failed
one,
.Er EROFS .
Other bits, including
.Dv MS_BIND
and
.Dv MS_MOVE ,
are refused.
A change of propagation alone,
.Dv MS_PRIVATE ,
.Dv MS_SLAVE ,
.Dv MS_SHARED
or
.Dv MS_UNBINDABLE ,
with
.Dv MS_REC
or without, succeeds and changes nothing, since a mount is seen in the
namespaces that had it when it was made and in none it is made in later.
.Pp
A mount is made in the caller's mount namespace alone, and an unmount
removes it from that namespace, and from the system when no other
namespace has it; see
.Xr unshare 2 .
See
.Xr mount 8
for the command interface.
.Pp
For
.Ql tmpfs ,
a file system in memory whose files go at the unmount, the source is
any string, conventionally
.Ql none
or
.Ql tmpfs ,
and is recorded as given; nothing is claimed.
Its root has mode 1777, or the octal mode an option
.Ql mode=
gives; the other options Linux's tmpfs takes are accepted and change
nothing.
For every other type, the source must name a published block device
under
.Pa /dev :
a disk or partition containing a volume,
.Pa /dev/bootpart ,
or an attached loop device.
The source string is used directly; a relative source is not resolved
against the working directory.
A read-only source makes the mount read-only regardless of
.Fa flags .
.Ss Argument checks and mounting
The handler checks for a current process and privilege before copying
any strings.
It copies the source, then the target, makes the target absolute,
then copies the type and options.
The source, target, type, and options must fit buffers of 64, 4096, 16,
and 128 bytes respectively, including the terminating null byte.
A relative target is joined to the current working directory, and an
empty one is
.Er ENOENT .
Repeated slashes and dot components are removed;
.Ql ..
is resolved by walking the parent directories.
.Pp
The VFS then performs these checks in order:
.Bl -enum
.It
Recognize the type, or return
.Er ENODEV .
.It
Reject unsupported flag bits with
.Er EINVAL .
.It
Resolve and hold the target, returning any path-resolution error.
.It
Require the absolute target path to fit 63 bytes, excluding the null
byte, or return
.Er ENAMETOOLONG .
.It
Reject the root directory and a directory of the synthetic
.Pa /dev
or
.Pa /proc
themselves with
.Er EINVAL ;
a directory of the tmpfs
.Pa /dev/shm
below them is a target like any other.
A mount of type
.Ql proc
over a directory of
.Pa /proc
succeeds and changes nothing, since every
.Pa /proc
shows the processes of its reader's PID namespace, as
.Ql unshare --mount-proc
would have it; one anywhere else fails with
.Er ENODEV .
.It
Reject the root of an already placed mount with
.Er EBUSY .
.It
Read the target's attributes and require a directory, or return
.Er ENOTDIR .
.It
Rebuild the target's path from the root of the mount table, the path
the mount table records whatever path named the target, a root set by
.Xr chroot 2
included, within the same 63-byte limit, or return
.Er ENAMETOOLONG .
.It
Under the parent directory's namespace lock, reject a covered or
reserved target with
.Er EBUSY .
.El
.Pp
The kernel then resolves the source device and claims its sector range.
An overlapping mount or write-capable raw descriptor prevents the
claim, even when another device name denotes the same sectors.
A loop device is identified by the device itself and permits one mount.
The claim remains until unmount.
.Pp
The mount table grows from eight slots as needed.
The kernel reserves a slot before running the file-system constructor,
holds the covered directory, and keeps a reference to its parent mount.
The mount becomes active only after the constructor succeeds.
A failure releases the claim and references acquired by the call.
The kernel mounts a tmpfs at
.Pa /dev/shm
at boot, with
.Dv MS_NOSUID
and
.Dv MS_NODEV .
.Pa /dev
is synthetic and has no directory to cover, so
.Pa /dev/shm
stands in its root directory by name, as
.Pa /dev
stands in the root's; a mount may be placed on a directory inside it.
.Pp
An active mount's source, target and type appear in
.Pa /proc/mounts
with
.Ql ro
or
.Ql rw
and whichever of
.Ql nosuid ,
.Ql nodev ,
.Ql noexec ,
.Ql noatime ,
.Ql nodiratime
and
.Ql relatime
are set; the silent flag and the file system's own options are not
shown.
KRFFS and ext2 ignore the option string; FAT32, exFAT and tmpfs parse
it.
.Ss Dirty-volume checks
Before a writable mount, FAT32 checks a dirty volume with limits of
32 directory levels including the root and 16 surplus tails.
Duplicate-name comparison requires each ordinary short entry to lie
within the first 4096 directory slots; long-name and deleted slots
count toward that position.
Exceeding any bound makes the mount read-only and logs the reason.
.Pp
KRFFS repair compares at most 4096 named reserved nodes.
A dirty volume with more fails a writable mount with
.Er EIO
before repair writes begin.
A read-only mount skips repair.
.Pp
An ext2 volume with its valid bit clear or error bit set is checked
before a writable mount.
A consistent volume can mount writable; a structural fault makes it
read-only.
.Ss Unmounting
.Fn umount2
requires
.Fa target
to resolve to the root of a mount.
A target that ends in a slash, or whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
must reach a directory, so a file named with a trailing slash returns
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as on Linux, where the same file named without one returns
.Er EINVAL .
.Fa flags
must be zero;
.Dv MNT_FORCE ,
.Dv MNT_DETACH ,
.Dv UMOUNT_NOFOLLOW ,
and all other nonzero values return
.Er EINVAL .
The root,
.Pa /dev ,
.Pa /proc ,
and
.Pa /dev/shm
are permanent and return
.Er EBUSY .
.Pp
The mount stops accepting new operations and waits for operations
already in progress to finish.
An open-file description, working directory, root directory set by
.Xr chroot 2 ,
program a process runs, or child mount in any process then makes it busy.
A loop device attached to a file on the mount also prevents unmounting.
.Pp
The file system syncs and prepares to unmount; the kernel writes dirty
cache buffers, flushes the device, and invalidates its cached buffers.
A tmpfs has no device and writes nothing; its unmount frees its files.
For a writable KRFFS volume, preparation flushes earlier writes to the
medium before clearing and separately flushing the dirty flag.
If clearing or flushing that flag fails, unmount returns
.Er EIO
and the mount becomes read-only.
A KRFFS volume made read-only before preparation keeps that flag set.
Only then does it release the file system, covered directory, and claim.
Failure before release restores the mount to active state.
.Pp
A mount whose device has been removed can be unmounted once its
references are released, without writes to the absent device.
If a removed ancestor prevents path lookup, the recorded target can
identify a unique active child mount below it.
A caller whose root
.Xr chroot 2
moved names the target from that root, and only a mount below the root
matches.
Children must be unmounted before their parents.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call number for
.Fn mount
is 21, with source in EBX, target in ECX, type in EDX, flags in ESI,
and data in EDI.
The number for
.Fn umount2
is 52, with target in EBX and flags in ECX.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success, or -1 on failure and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /proc/mounts
.It Pa /proc/mounts
Active mounts with the flags listed above.
Mounts whose devices have been removed are omitted.
.El
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
An ancestor directory denies search permission.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The target is covered, reserved, or in a mount undergoing a transition;
the source overlaps a claimed range or an open raw writer;
the source device is already mounted; or an unmount is prevented by
references or cache buffers still held or dirty.
Unmounting a permanent mount also returns this error.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A required string pointer is null or a string cannot be read from
user memory.
.It Bq Er EFBIG
A KRFFS source device exceeds the size the file system can address.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A flag is unsupported, the mount target is the root or in a synthetic
namespace, the source of a type with a device is not a supported block
device, or the unmount target is not a mount root.
A constructor also returns this error for invalid options or volume
structure it cannot accept.
.It Bq Er EIO
Path lookup or file-system I/O failed, the backing device was removed,
or unmount writeback or device flushing failed.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Target resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
A copied string or joined path exceeds its buffer, the mount target
exceeds 63 bytes, or a path component or symbolic-link expansion is
too long.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
The file-system type is unknown.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
A target component does not exist, the source device is not published,
or the selected loop device is unattached.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
Memory for path resolution, a mount record, or file-system state is
unavailable, or the sector-claim table is full.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
The mount target or a directory component is not a directory, or the
unmount target ends in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
and reaches something else.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller's effective user ID is not 0, and it is not privileged over its
own mount namespace, or is and mounts a type other than
.Ql proc ,
.Ql sysfs
or
.Ql tmpfs ,
or remounts.
.It Bq Er EROFS
An exFAT unmount could not write its metadata after an earlier write
failure.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mkdir 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr loop 4 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr mount 8
